### What Actually Causes Cold Start Latency

A cold start has three components stacked on top of each other: (1) provisioning a new execution environment, which you have essentially no control over, (2) initializing the JS runtime and loading/parsing your function's bundle, and (3) running any top-level (module-scope) initialization code before the handler can respond to the first request. Almost all of the optimization leverage you have as an application developer is in (2) and (3) — you can't make the platform provision faster, but you can make there be dramatically less code to load and less work to do before the first response.

### Optimizations, in priority order

**1. Reduce what's in the serverless function's bundle at all.** Every import at the top of a Route Handler or Server Component that touches the database, or a heavy SDK, gets bundled into that function and parsed on cold start even if the request doesn't need it. Split routes so unrelated functionality doesn't share a bundle, and check what's actually landing in each function with `next build`'s bundle output.

**2. Defer expensive initialization out of module scope.** This is the highest-leverage fix and the most commonly missed one:

```typescript
// Bad — runs on every cold start, even for requests that don't need the DB
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
const db = new PrismaClient(); // connects/initializes at module load

// Better — lazy singleton, initialized only when actually used
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
let db: PrismaClient | undefined;
function getDb() {
  if (!db) db = new PrismaClient();
  return db;
}
```

**3. Reuse connections across invocations where the platform allows it.** Serverless platforms often keep an execution environment warm between consecutive invocations (just not guaranteed, and not on a true cold start) — a module-scope variable set to `undefined` initially and populated lazily, as above, is naturally reused on warm invocations instead of reconnecting every time.

**4. Prefer connection-pooling-aware or HTTP-based database drivers** for serverless specifically (e.g. Prisma Accelerate, Neon's serverless driver, PlanetScale's HTTP driver) — a raw TCP connection pool doesn't behave well when instances are spun up and torn down constantly; each cold start potentially means a fresh, slow connection handshake against a database that also has a hard cap on concurrent connections.

**5. Tree-shake and avoid barrel-file imports of large packages.** Importing `import { z } from 'zod'` is fine; importing a whole icon library's index (`import * as Icons from 'a-huge-icon-set'` or even `import { SomeIcon } from 'a-huge-icon-set'` without that package supporting proper tree-shaking) can silently pull in far more code than used, inflating parse time.

**6. Move genuinely edge-appropriate, lightweight logic to the Edge Runtime** (see the Edge Runtime question) — its isolate-based cold start is an order of magnitude faster than a full Node.js process spin-up, but this only helps for code that doesn't need Node-specific APIs or TCP database connections.

**7. Combine with ISR/caching so cold starts happen less often on the hot path.** If a route's output is cacheable, a cache hit at the CDN/Full Route Cache layer means the serverless function isn't invoked at all for most requests — the fastest cold start is the one that never happens.

**Common pitfall:** optimizing bundle size and imports while leaving a `new PrismaClient()` or similar heavy client instantiated at module scope in a function that's rarely invoked — the bundle-size wins get partially eaten by the exact same class of problem (expensive work happening before the handler can respond) that the bundle-size optimization was meant to fix.

